1382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 597 0 R
/T (1. Position the child on their back on a firm, hard surface. 2. Move any thick clothing away from the chest \(Figure 19a\).  3. Place the heel of one hand on the lower half of the breastbone \(Figure 19b\). 4. Push straight down approximately at least one third the depth of the chest. In most children,      this will be about two inches \(5 cm\). Compressions should be delivered at a rate of 100 to      120 beats per minute \(Figure 19c\). 5. Let the chest recoil to its normal position after every compression.)
/E (1. Position the child on their back on a firm, hard surface. 2. Move any thick clothing away from the chest \(Figure 19a\).  3. Place the heel of one hand on the lower half of the breastbone \(Figure 19b\). 4. Push straight down approximately at least one third the depth of the chest. In most children,      this will be about two inches \(5 cm\). Compressions should be delivered at a rate of 100 to      120 beats per minute \(Figure 19c\). 5. Let the chest recoil to its normal position after every compression.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
1383 0 obj
<<
